        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "19c95d6e-2d17-5932-a791-d415dbeac3f3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: John Bell Hood, William T. Sherman\n- Events: Franklin–Nashville Campaign, Atlanta Campaign, American Civil War\n- Organizations: Confederate Army of Tennessee, Confederate States Army\n- Places: Alabama, Tennessee, northwestern Georgia, Atlanta\n- Roles: Confederate general, brigade commander, division commander, lieutenant general, major general\n\nFacts:\n- The Franklin–Nashville Campaign was a series of battles in the Western Theater of the American Civil War fought from September 18 to December 27, 1864, in Alabama, Tennessee, and northwestern Georgia.\n- During the Franklin–Nashville Campaign, the Confederate Army of Tennessee under Lt. Gen. John Bell Hood drove north from Atlanta and threatened Maj. Gen. William T. Sherman’s lines of communications and central Tennessee.\n- John Bell Hood, born on June 1 or June 29, 1831, was a Confederate general during the American Civil War.\n- John Bell Hood was regarded as one of the Confederate States Army’s best brigade and division commanders, but he became increasingly ineffective in larger independent commands late in the war.\n- John Bell Hood’s career and reputation were marred by decisive defeats while leading an army in the Atlanta Campaign and the Franklin–Nashville Campaign.\n- John Bell Hood died on August 30, 1879.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "034266e1-0965-5fa5-9dd9-ea257bd7c04d",
        "text": "Page: Franklin–Nashville Campaign. Sentence: The Franklin–Nashville Campaign, also known as Hood's Tennessee Campaign, was a series of battles in the Western Theater, conducted from September 18 to December 27, 1864, in Alabama, Tennessee, and northwestern Georgia during the American Civil War.\nPage: Franklin–Nashville Campaign. Sentence: The Confederate Army of Tennessee under Lt. Gen. John Bell Hood drove north from Atlanta, threatening Maj. Gen. William T. Sherman's lines of communications and central Tennessee.\nPage: John Bell Hood. Sentence: John Bell Hood (June 1 or June 29, 1831 – August 30, 1879) was a Confederate general during the American Civil War.\nPage: John Bell Hood. Sentence: Arguably one of the best brigade and division commanders in the Confederate States Army, Hood gradually became increasingly ineffective as he was promoted to lead larger, independent commands late in the war; his career and reputation were marred by his decisive defeats leading an army in the Atlanta Campaign and the Franklin–Nashville Campaign.",
